nfty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186344421 :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" "B={\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}"] [:db/cas 17592186344424 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4+6\\text{i} & 7-5\\text{i} \\\\ 8+6\\text{i} & -1-13\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}4+6\\text{i} & 7-5\\text{i} \\\\ 8+6\\text{i} & -1-13\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186344425 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}77 \\\\ 42 \\\\ -84\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}77 \\\\ 42 \\\\ -84\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186344426 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186344441 :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]}" "B={\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix}}"] [:db/cas 17592186344442 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -10 & -5 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -10 & -5 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186344444 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} & 1+\\text{i} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1+2\\text{i} & 1+\\text{i} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186344445 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 23 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 23 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186344944 :question/worked-solution "By definition, $u\\in Π$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sb+tc & =u \\\\ s[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 19 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}s+t=3 \\\\ 3s-2t=19 \\\\ 6s+4t=-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 19 \\\\ 6 & 4 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 10 \\\\ 6 & 4 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-6{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -28\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -28\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -32\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=-32,$ which has no solution.\n\nSo, there are no values of $s$ and $t$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sb+tc=u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $u$ does *not* lie on the plane $Π.$" "By definition, $u\\in Π$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sb+tc & =u \\\\ s\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 19 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}s+t=3 \\\\ 3s-2t=19 \\\\ 6s+4t=-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 19 \\\\ 6 & 4 & -10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 10 \\\\ 6 & 4 & -10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-6{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -28\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -28\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -32\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=-32,$ which has no solution.\n\nSo, there are no values of $s$ and $t$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sb+tc=u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $u$ does *not* lie on the plane $Π.$"] [:db/cas 17592186344945 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 5 & 15\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 5 & 15\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 5 & 15\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 5 & 15\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 5 & 15\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 5 & 15\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186344946 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 12 & -18 & 12 \\\\ -10 & -20 & 30 & -20\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 12 & -18 & 12 \\\\ -10 & -20 & 30 & -20\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 12 & -18 & 12 \\\\ -10 & -20 & 30 & -20\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -10 & -20 & 30 & -20\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+10{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 12 & -18 & 12 \\\\ -10 & -20 & 30 & -20\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 12 & -18 & 12 \\\\ -10 & -20 & 30 & -20\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 12 & -18 & 12 \\\\ -10 & -20 & 30 & -20\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -10 & -20 & 30 & -20\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+10{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186344948 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -10 & -5 & 10 \\\\ -10 & 100 & 50 & -100 \\\\ -4 & 40 & 20 & -40\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -10 & -5 & 10 \\\\ -10 & 100 & 50 & -100 \\\\ -4 & 40 & 20 & -40\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+10{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -10 & -5 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 40 & 20 & -40\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -10 & -5 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -10 & -5 & 10 \\\\ -10 & 100 & 50 & -100 \\\\ -4 & 40 & 20 & -40\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -10 & -5 & 10 \\\\ -10 & 100 & 50 & -100 \\\\ -4 & 40 & 20 & -40\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+10{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -10 & -5 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 40 & 20 & -40\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -10 & -5 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186344949 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -6 & 15 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 12 & -30 & 6 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 30 & -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -6 & 15 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 12 & -30 & 6 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 30 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 12 & -30 & 6 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 30 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 30 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+6{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y-5z=1 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final two equations, we can remove these equations from the system. \n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have two free variables, $y,$ and $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y$ and $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1-2y+5z \\\\ y=y \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ and $z$ (the free variables) are arbitrary numbers.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1-2y+5z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -6 & 15 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 12 & -30 & 6 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 30 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -6 & 15 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 12 & -30 & 6 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 30 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 12 & -30 & 6 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 30 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 30 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+6{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y-5z=1 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final two equations, we can remove these equations from the system. \n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have two free variables, $y,$ and $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y$ and $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1-2y+5z \\\\ y=y \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ and $z$ (the free variables) are arbitrary numbers.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1-2y+5z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186344950 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 12 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 12 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 12 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y+4z=1 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final two equations, we can remove these equations from the system. \n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have two free variables, $y,$ and $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y$ and $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1-2y-4z \\\\ y=y \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ and $z$ (the free variables) are arbitrary numbers.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1-2y-4z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 12 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 12 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 12 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y+4z=1 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final two equations, we can remove these equations from the system. \n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have two free variables, $y,$ and $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y$ and $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1-2y-4z \\\\ y=y \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ and $z$ (the free variables) are arbitrary numbers.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1-2y-4z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186344951 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2 & -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the row echelon form above, we have only two pivots (in the $1$st and $2$nd columns). To transform the matrix to reduced row echelon form, we need to create zeros above the pivots only. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the row echelon form above, we have only two pivots (in the $1$st and $2$nd columns). To transform the matrix to reduced row echelon form, we need to create zeros above the pivots only. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186344952 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 & 5 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 10 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ into reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 & 5 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 10 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 10 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 5 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 5 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the row echelon form above, we have only two pivots (in the $1$st and $2$nd columns). To transform the matrix to reduced row echelon form, we need to create zeros above the pivots only. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+5z=-3 \\\\ y+z=-1 \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation, we can remove this equation from the system. \n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-3-5z \\\\ y=-z-1 \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3-5z \\\\ -z-1 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 & 5 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 10 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ into reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 & 5 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 10 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 10 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the row echelon form above, we have only two pivots (in the $1$st and $2$nd columns). To transform the matrix to reduced row echelon form, we need to create zeros above the pivots only. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+5z=-3 \\\\ y+z=-1 \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation, we can remove this equation from the system. \n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-3-5z \\\\ y=-z-1 \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3-5z \\\\ -z-1 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186344953 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -12 & 12 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 16 & -9 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -6 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ into reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -12 & 12 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 16 & -9 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -6 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 16 & -9 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -6 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -6 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the row echelon form above, we have only two pivots (in the $1$st and $2$nd columns). To transform the matrix to reduced row echelon form, we need to create zeros above the pivots only. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -21 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-21z=4 \\\\ y+6z=-1 \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation, we can remove this equation from the system. \n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=21z+4 \\\\ y=-6z-1 \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}21z+4 \\\\ -6z-1 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -12 & 12 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 16 & -9 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ into reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -12 & 12 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 16 & -9 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 16 & -9 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the row echelon form above, we have only two pivots (in the $1$st and $2$nd columns). To transform the matrix to reduced row echelon form, we need to create zeros above the pivots only. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -21 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-21z=4 \\\\ y+6z=-1 \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation, we can remove this equation from the system. \n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=21z+4 \\\\ y=-6z-1 \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}21z+4 \\\\ -6z-1 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186344956 :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first try to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & \\frac{3}{2} & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means that  we can find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, since there are infinitely many solutions, we conclude that the system is *consistent dependent*. \n\nAs a result, only statement III is true." "To classify the system, we first try to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & \\frac{3}{2} & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means that  we can find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, since there are infinitely many solutions, we conclude that the system is *consistent dependent*. \n\nAs a result, only statement III is true."] [:db/cas 17592186344957 :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first try to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince any numbers $x$ and $y$ are solutions to the third equation $0x+0y=0$, we can remove this equation from the system. So, the corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-2y=1 \\\\ 7y=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $y=-\\frac{3}{7}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx-2(-\\frac{3}{7})=1\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{7}.\n$$\n\n \nSo, we conclude that the system is consistent independent. \n\nAs a result, only statement II is true." "To classify the system, we first try to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is \n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince any numbers $x$ and $y$ are solutions to the third equation $0x+0y=0$, we can remove this equation from the system. So, the corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-2y=1 \\\\ 7y=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $y=-\\frac{3}{7}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx-2(-\\frac{3}{7})=1\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{7}.\n$$\n\n \nSo, we conclude that the system is consistent independent. \n\nAs a result, only statement II is true."]]
